@startuml d6_embedded_jsonb
!include _style.puml
title D6 — embedded-jsonb  (the child table folded into its parent)

entity "charity" as c {
  * charity_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  * name : TEXT
  * country : TEXT
  * founded_on : DATE
}
entity "person" as p {
  * person_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  * charity_id : BIGINT <<FK>>
  * full_name : TEXT
  * email : TEXT
  * joined_at : TIMESTAMPTZ
  --
  <back:#E7DDF5>* donations : JSONB  — the whole child table</back>
}
c ||--o{ p

note right of p
  **array element shape**
  {
    "i": donation_id,
    "a": amount_cents,
    "c": currency,
    "t": donated_at,
    "n": note
  }
  Appended in chronological order, so
  donations->0 is the first donation and
  donations->-1 is the last — both
  reachable with no unnesting at all.

  Single-character keys on purpose: JSONB
  stores every key of every element, so
  key names multiply by the element count.
end note

note bottom of p
  **+ person_charity_idx** (charity_id)
  **+ person_donations_gin** GIN (donations jsonb_path_ops)

  There is no donation table, so there is no
  index on donated_at, on amount, or on
  donation_id. A GIN index can answer
  "which document contains this?" but it can
  neither order nor aggregate across documents.

  Wins: everything about ONE person is one row.
  Loses: everything ACROSS persons unnests the
  whole table; and appending one donation
  rewrites the entire row, so under MVCC the
  cost of a write grows with the history it
  already has.
end note
@enduml
